'''Walton and Ivythorn Hills''' is a {{convert|34.9|ha}} [[biological Site of Special Scientific Interest]] near [[Street, Somerset|Street]] at the south-eastern end of the [[Polden Hills]] in the English county of [[Somerset]]. '''Walton Hill''' is south of the village of [[Walton, Somerset|Walton]], and '''Ivythorn Hill''' is south of Street. Notified in 1953, the site is now owned and managed by the [[National Trust for Places of Historic Interest or Natural Beauty|National Trust]] who acquired the freehold in 1988.

The hills which rise to {{convert|80|m}} above [[Ordnance datum]] (mean sea level) are made up of [[limestone]], rheatic clay and [[Keuper marl]] covered with [[calcareous grassland]], [[scrubland]] and [[Coppicing|coppiced]] woodland. A quarry existed in the [[Middle Ages]]. This supports a variety of [[Butterfly|Butterflies]], [[Leafhopper]]s, [[Spider]]s, [[Stratiomyidae|Soldier Flies]] and [[Tettigonia viridissima|Great Green Bush Crickets]] living on the vegetation. Notable buildings include [[Ivythorne Manor]], which was originally built for [[Glastonbury Abbey]], Walton windmill and a small [[sanatorium]] which became the first [[hostel|youth hostel]] in Somerset. In 1977 a short lived [[hippie]] camp was set up on Ivythorne Hill. The camp was a Free Festival to celebrate  7/7/77. The festival was attended by an estimated 3,000 people and was basically a one-day affair, with some people arriving early and the hangers on eventually evicted by the council.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.ukrockfestivals.com/glasto77.html|title = Glastonbury free festival 1977}}</ref>

==Geography==
The [[Polden Hills]] a long, low ridge, extending for {{convert|10|mi|km}}, and separated from the [[Mendip Hills]], to which they are nearly parallel, by a marshy tract, known as the [[Somerset Levels]]. The ridge is underlain by [[Blue Lias]] with alternating [[stratum|strata]] of [[limestone]], rheatic clay and [[Keuper marl]].<ref name=sssicitation/>

At the highest point on Walton Hill, which reaches {{convert|80|m}} above [[Ordnance datum]] (mean sea level),<ref name=sssicitation/> there is a topograph or orientation stone used as a [[Triangulation station|triangulation point]] and highlighting key features of the surrounding landscape including the [[Somerset Levels]] and the [[Admiral Hood Monument]].<ref>{{cite web|title=TP9460 - Walton Hill|url=http://trigpointing.uk/trig/9460|publisher=Trig Pointing UK|access-date=24 May 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Assessment of Special Landscape Features|url=http://www.mendip.gov.uk/CHttpHandler.ashx?id=1022&p=0|publisher=Mendip District Council|access-date=25 May 2015|archive-date=25 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150525150215/http://www.mendip.gov.uk/CHttpHandler.ashx?id=1022&p=0|url-status=dead}}</ref> It forms the starting point for the {{convert|6|mi}} long footpath known as the Polden Way.<ref>{{cite news|title=The heart of the West: a land of heroes and windmills|url=http://www.westerndailypress.co.uk/heart-West-land-heroes-windmills/story-12331991-detail/story.html|access-date=25 May 2015|work=Western Daily Press|date=3 February 2011|archive-date=25 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150525150755/http://www.westerndailypress.co.uk/heart-West-land-heroes-windmills/story-12331991-detail/story.html|url-status=dead}}</ref>

==History==
[[File:The old mill on Walton Hill - geograph.org.uk - 1508528.jpg|thumb|left|Walton windmill]]

A quarry on Ivythorn Hill produced stone for Street Manor in 1403.<ref name=bho/> [[Ivythorne Manor]] is a Grade II* [[listed building]] which was built as a monastic dwelling in the medieval era and rebuilt in 1488 by John Selwood, Abbot of [[Glastonbury Abbey]]. It later became a [[country house]] and was revised and extended from the 16th to 20th centuries by the Marshall and Sydenham families which owned it.<ref>{{NHLE|desc=Ivythorn Manor|num=1176171|access-date=25 May 2015}}</ref> Within the grounds is a [[dovecote]] which was probably built around 1578 for John Sydenham.<ref>{{NHLE|desc=Dovecote in Grounds East of Ivythorn Manor|num=1345094|access-date=25 May 2015}}</ref>

Walton windmill on Walton Hill was described as "new-erected" in 1741, although there had been a mill erected on the site in 1342.<ref name=bho>{{cite web|last1=Siraut|first1=M.C|last2=Thacker|first2=A.T.|last3=Wliiamson|first3=Elizabeth|title=Parishes: Walton. A History of the County of Somerset: Volume 9, Glastonbury and Street|url=http://www.british-history.ac.uk/vch/som/vol9/pp198-212|website=British History Online|publisher=Victoria County History|access-date=24 May 2015}}</ref> It was worked until 1906.<ref name=Watts>{{cite book | first =and Watts, Martin| last = Coulthard, Alfred J| year = 1978| title = Windmills of Somerset and the men who worked them| pages= 62| publisher = The Research Publishing Co| location = London| isbn = 0-7050-0060-5}}</ref> It is now a Grade II [[listed building]] and private residence.<ref>{{NHLE |num=1058724 |desc=Walton Windmill |access-date=8 February 2009}}</ref><ref>{{NHLE|desc=Walton Windmill|num=1058724|access-date=24 May 2015}}</ref> There was also a windmill on Ivythorne Hill.<ref>{{cite web|title=Windmill site, Ivy Thorn Wood, Street|url=http://www.somersetheritage.org.uk/record/24707|website=Somerset Historic Environment Record|publisher=Somerset County Council|access-date=25 May 2015}}</ref>

The shoe manufacturer [[C. & J. Clark]], through the Society of Friends, had a small [[sanatorium]] and [[Sanatorium|convalescent home]] on Ivythorn Hill overlooking Street. In 1931, this [[Swiss chalet style|chalet style]] building was leased to the [[Youth Hostels Association (England & Wales)|Youth Hostel Association]] and became the first [[hostel|youth hostel]] in Somerset. It is still used for this purpose.<ref>{{cite web|title=Youth Hostel, Street|url=http://www.yha.org.uk/find-accommodation/south-west-england/hostels/Street/index.aspx|publisher=Youth Hostel Association|access-date=24 May 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last1=Siraut|first1=M. C.|last2=Thacker|first2=A. T.|last3=Williamson|first3=Elizabeth|title=Parishes: Street In A History of the County of Somerset: Volume 9, Glastonbury and Street.|url=http://www.british-history.ac.uk/vch/som/vol9/pp165-198|website=British History Online|publisher=Victoria County History|access-date=25 May 2015}}</ref>

[[File:Street Youth Hostel - geograph.org.uk - 1178893.jpg|thumb|The [[Youth Hostels Association (England & Wales)|youth hostel]]]]
This site is owned and managed by the [[National Trust for Places of Historic Interest or Natural Beauty|National Trust]]. They acquired the freehold of {{convert|0.248|ha}} of Ivythorn Hill in 1988 from Street Estates, which followed {{convert|16.606|ha}} of Walton Hill in 1940 from Polden Farms Ltd and the initial {{convert|18.751|ha}} of Ivythorn Hill and Wood in 1919 from [[Hood baronets|Baron St Audries]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Acquisitions Up to December 2011|url=http://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/cs/Satellite?blobcol=urldata&blobheader=application%2Fpdf&blobheadername1=Content-Disposition&blobheadername2=MDT-Type&blobheadername3=Content-Type&blobheadervalue1=inline%3B+filename%3D297%252F965%252Fnt_acquisitions_dec2011-2%252C2.pdf&blobheadervalue2=abinary%3B+charset%3DUTF-8&blobheadervalue3=application%2Fpdf&blobkey=id&blobtable=MungoBlobs&blobwhere=1349117284549&ssbinary=true|publisher=National Trust|access-date=24 May 2015|format=PDF}}</ref>

In 1977 Ivythorne Hill was the site of a [[hippie]] camp following [[summer solstice]] celebrations at [[Stonehenge]]. They were evicted by [[Mendip District Council]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Looking Back: Calls for army to deal with Street Hill hippy invasion in 1977|url=http://www.centralsomersetgazette.co.uk/army-deal-hippy-invasion/story-22872803-detail/story.html|access-date=25 May 2015|work=Central Somerset Gazette|date=7 September 2014|archive-date=8 September 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140908094259/http://www.centralsomersetgazette.co.uk/army-deal-hippy-invasion/story-22872803-detail/story.html|url-status=dead}}</ref>

==Ecology==

Walton and Ivythorn Hills are covered by a variety of semi-natural habitats. These include unimproved [[calcareous grassland]], [[scrubland]] and [[Coppicing|coppiced]] woodland including [[Acer campestre|field maple]] and [[Fraxinus|Ash]]. The range of habitats are home to many species of [[invertebrate]]. [[Butterfly|Butterflies]], [[Leafhopper]]s, [[Spider]]s and [[Stratiomyidae|Soldier Flies]] are particularly well represented. There is also a population of [[Tettigonia viridissima|Great Green Bush Cricket]] (''Tettigonia viridissima'').<ref name=sssicitation>{{cite web | title=Walton and Ivythorn Hills SSSI citation sheet | publisher=English Nature | url=http://www.english-nature.org.uk/citation/citation_photo/1001144.pdf | access-date=24 May 2015}}</ref>

Between 2011 and 2013 a project was undertaken to increase the population of [[Large blue]] butterflies on the Polden Hills. This included planting [[Thymus serpyllum|Wild Thyme]] and managing the grazing on Walton Hill.<ref>{{cite web|title=Expanding the Large Blue Landscape in the Polden Hills. Project Update|url=http://butterfly-conservation.org/files/1.elbph_project-update-june2013.pdf|publisher=Butterfly Conservation|access-date=24 May 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Large Blue butterfly at Collard Hill|url=http://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/article-1356396112443/|publisher=National Trust|access-date=25 May 2015}}</ref>

